# Store text as LF, always.
#
# "text=auto" is not enough here. It asks Git to guess, and Git's guess is that a file holding a
# lone carriage return, one not paired with a newline, is binary. It then stops normalising that
# file's line endings and commits it whole, as CRLF. A sweep whose block regex matched \n but not
# \r\n left exactly such a stray on about a thousand pages, and every rebuild since has been
# committing ninety thousand lines of line-ending churn on top of a dozen real ones.
#
# An explicit "text" skips the guess. Windows checkouts still get CRLF in the working tree, which
# is what core.autocrlf is for; the repository keeps LF.
*.html   text
*.css    text
*.js     text
*.cjs    text
*.mjs    text
*.json   text
*.xml    text
*.md     text
*.txt    text
*.svg    text
*.py     text

*.webp   binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.png    binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
